    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nWith ui2, when I run second command that produce message, while the expanded message from previous command still visible, the focus is silently moved into the pager window. So next keys (`j`, `k`, ...) is operating in the pager, but in my mind I still working in my buffer window.\n\nThis is contradict with documented mental model: `:help g<` / `:help pager` (message.txt) only document `g<` as the way to show/enter the pager. The auto-enter behavior is not documented anywhere.\n\nI try to trace the mechanism:\n\n1. First command message exceed 'cmdheight' → cmdline get temporarily expanded, `cmd_on_key` armed. Focus unchanged (good).\n2. When typing `:` the expanded message is keep opened; `cmdline.lua` `cmdline_show()` sets `cmd.expand = 1` and move the message to dialog window.\n3. Then the second command message is force-routed to pager (`messages.lua` `msg_show`: `tgt = ui.cmd.expand > 0 and 'pager' or tgt`), and `set_pos('pager')` will call `enter_pager()` → `nvim_set_current_win(ui.wins.pager)`.\n\nSo it seems this is intentional (introduced in #38272 to mimic UI1 block-mode/more-prompt for consecutive commands). But I have two observation why it is still surprising:\n\n- The legacy more-prompt also consume keys, but it *self-terminates* (reaching the end / `<Esc>` will return you to the buffer). The ui2 pager keeps the focus until you press `q` or switch window yourself.\n- It also trigger even when second message is only single line that actually fit the cmdline (`:echo 'hi'` after an overflowing `:ls` also steal the focus).\n\nCursor position and file contents is irrelevant; the only precondition is the first message taller than 'cmdheight'.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```\nnvim --clean\n:lua require('vim._core.ui2').enable {}\n:e any_file\n:ls        \" output taller than 'cmdheight' → expanded cmdline, focus stays in buffer\n:ls        \" (or :echo 'hi') → pager opens AND become the current window\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe pager should not grab the focus implicitly. My suggestion: open the pager *unfocused* when consecutive commands emit messages, and enter it only via `g<` (like documented). There is already precedent in-tree for interacting with unfocused message float: `dialog_on_key` in `messages.lua` implements unfocused paging (`<PageUp>`/`<PageDown>` etc.) for the dialog window; maybe same pattern can serve the auto-opened pager also.\n\nHappy to work on a PR if some direction is agreed.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-a3eff66e31\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nN/A (ui2)\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nLinux 6.18.40-2-lts\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nkitty\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nbuild from repo\n",
